{"title":"2024 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 18","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Ruler_Tool)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Ruler Tool)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_DDM\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by DDM</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_Math-X_(First_fully_understand_the_problem!!!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by Math-X (First fully understand the problem!!!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_(A_Clever_Explanation_You’ll_Get_Instantly)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 (A Clever Explanation You’ll Get Instantly)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_3_(super_clear!)_by_Power_Solve\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 3 (super clear!) by Power Solve</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_4_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 4 by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_5_by_NiuniuMaths_(Easy_to_understand!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 5 by NiuniuMaths (Easy to understand!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Central_Valley_Math_Circle_(Goes_through_full_thought_process)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">14</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Central Valley Math Circle (Goes through full thought process)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-15\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_6_by_OmegaLearn.org_(Easy_To_Understand)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">15</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 6 by OmegaLearn.org (Easy To Understand)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-16\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_7_by_CosineMethod_[🔥Fast_and_Easy🔥]\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">16</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 7 by CosineMethod [🔥Fast and Easy🔥]</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-17\"><a href=\"#Video_Solutions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">17</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solutions</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-18\"><a href=\"#Video_solution_by_TheNeuralMathAcademy\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">18</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video solution by TheNeuralMathAcademy</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-19\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_SquishBJ_(AIME_Qualifier)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">19</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by SquishBJ (AIME Qualifier)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-20\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">20</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Three concentric circles centered at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> have radii of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> lie on the largest circle. The region between the two smaller circles is shaded, as is the portion of the region between the two larger circles bounded by central angles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/d/76d9b534de592b2a7fc63b97e1f0faab799204e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BOC$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, as shown in the figure below. Suppose the shaded and unshaded regions are equal in area. What is the measure of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/a/b2a2341949ae4f66400feedc1504aa06cb814506.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{BOC}$\" width=\"56\" height=\"12\" /> in degrees?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/9/439c8e9a56d8540a55cf0ba7052f87041811d6be.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(150); import graph;  draw(circle((0,0),3)); real radius = 3; real angleStart = -54;  // starting angle of the sector real angleEnd = 54;  // ending angle of the sector label(&quot;$O$&quot;,(0,0),W); pair O = (0, 0); filldraw(arc(O, radius, angleStart, angleEnd)--O--cycle, gray); filldraw(circle((0,0),2),gray); filldraw(circle((0,0),1),white); draw((1.763,2.427)--(0,0)--(1.763,-2.427)); label(&quot;$B$&quot;,(1.763,2.427),NE); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,(1.763,-2.427),SE);  [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"252\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/a/5daef0d52f10f338555e773c30c9f43f72062934.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 108\\qquad\\textbf{(B) } 120\\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 135\\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 144\\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 150$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"446\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/d/a4d8d4e596ffdd27849e6ff038790f085095e950.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\angle{BOC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"93\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>We see that the shaded region is the inner ring plus a sector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/6/7a66181795ab222ad9ff38cb534dead38e5a47de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"15\" /> of the outer ring. Using the formula for the area of a circle (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/2/3a29e7889fad76777233bc77041cf045d9fb6f15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = \\pi r^2$\" width=\"63\" height=\"15\" />), we find that the area of the shaded region is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/a/53aa790bf0715c704fb56aef255156bd038de798.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left( 4 \\pi - \\pi \\right)+\\frac{x}{360} \\left( 9 \\pi - 4 \\pi \\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"202\" height=\"34\" />. This simplifies to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/7/2e7ed8ebadafe4ffd75e49c0434c005623109726.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\pi + \\frac{x}{360}(5 \\pi)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"110\" height=\"34\" />.\n</p><p>The unshaded portion is comprised of the smallest circle plus the sector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/1/cd13c93fe3daeaeab846d0bd3c835d3263d3c3c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(360-x)^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"83\" height=\"20\" /> of the outer ring, which evaluates to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/d/aad2a668b9ac29b36f90095f96e456a4a880cccf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi + \\frac{360-x}{360}(5 \\pi)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"133\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>We are told these are equal. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/6/0568e19c908bbacefd95a223cc4f8d360901a9af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\pi + \\frac{x}{360}(5 \\pi) = \\pi + \\frac{360-x}{360}(5 \\pi)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"265\" height=\"38\" />. Solving for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> reveals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/a/6da65274eadba6df7db42ddfac0662c2fec7a34e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\boxed{\\textbf{(A) } 108}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"111\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~MrThinker\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/a/55a64b33750afe51aa0bbd57df78838b96ddd094.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\quad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"2\" />~Dash11 (Edited coefficient of pi. Credit goes to MrThinker for the solution and explanation.)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/a/55a64b33750afe51aa0bbd57df78838b96ddd094.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\quad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"2\" />~Minor edit by Quetzalcoatlus\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that for the 3rd most outer ring of the circle, the ratio of the shaded region to non-shaded region is the ratio of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/a/b2a2341949ae4f66400feedc1504aa06cb814506.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{BOC}$\" width=\"56\" height=\"12\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/a/58a663b6d847abbf0331566076fd3207081c70d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$360-\\angle{BOC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"108\" height=\"16\" />. With that, all we need to do is solve for the shaded region.\n</p><p>The inner most circle has radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and the second circle has radius 2. Therefore, the first shaded area has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/c/bcc85da20d3827c17d80e6d58f31f351dec691e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 \\pi - \\pi = 3 \\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"100\" height=\"15\" /> area. The circle has total area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/e/4eecdd4830005f7b13a04a16c1019a4c3dac062e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9 \\pi$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" />, so the other shaded region must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/6/716c76ec8d42e7e18f85e1552f8a0dce821b23c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.5 \\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"36\" height=\"14\" /> area, as the non-shaded and shaded area is equivalent. So for the 3rd outer ring, the total area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/8/d08c79ccbd4051b976d14aab32fc678f3dba6ced.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9 \\pi - 4 \\pi = 5 \\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"109\" height=\"15\" />, so the non-shaded part of the outer ring is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/e/4ee781d5a43fee6b3651d6bb4fee93114eb00d43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5 \\pi - 1.5 \\pi = 3.5 \\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"137\" height=\"15\" />. \n</p><p>Now as said before, the ratio of these two areas is the ratio of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/a/b2a2341949ae4f66400feedc1504aa06cb814506.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{BOC}$\" width=\"56\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/e/ece522a50398791f077d502a8ab8d4f9d5144792.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$360 - \\angle{BOC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"108\" height=\"16\" />. So, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/0/1f010102d18634b6fca2e68f592a2282187450f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3.5}{1.5} = \\frac{7}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"66\" height=\"38\" />. We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/8/9c810ebbfe8eb57cf89c6ff82e18bfd028ec9ae5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7x:3x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"56\" height=\"14\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/5/2b58cfea8e377df3127dc563f907fbf5d7216aad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7x+3x = 360$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"115\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/4/dd4744c45e8754d33c1461fc0eae806ed9ca39d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 36$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"55\" height=\"14\" />, so our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/c/77cb7f3941b0d35cec28d8fd8e4c936a221a926e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3x = 108, \\boxed{(A) 108}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"147\" height=\"32\" />.\n</p><p>~By someone unknown\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Ruler_Tool.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Ruler_Tool)\">Solution 3 (Ruler Tool)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Ruler Tool)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The AMC 8 test allows a ruler tool that you can rotate and drag. You can use the tool to make a straight segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> passing through point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> (which we know is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/b/5cb1b41850ab676e4d1cf15c69b1609af4befbc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> degrees), and we let the angle of desire be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />. We can estimate that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/6/ed6c05e52094482deffbfe6b4ee027a1a88d0a46.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180-x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"62\" height=\"15\" /> is just about <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/1/651ba418a810d971dbc8a326d49a71fe1541fae0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> degrees short of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> itself, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/c/c5c00841a1da777772cfc8e2f115458bbdfca1aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-30=180-x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"137\" height=\"15\" />, solving gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/6/0f65a6a2ea1526068ba5abba5114d38c3034d3b8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=105$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"61\" height=\"13\" />, therefore the closest answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/c/85c89a7937c4aa9a8b0e6f29840884a56b9dd138.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(A)}\\,108}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"73\" height=\"30\" />. \n</p>\n<ul><li>Note: This isn't the most accurate method to use on AMC 8s, this is just a quick method if time is short or you do not know how to do the problem and want to guess at it with your best shot.</li></ul>\n<p>~By someone unknown\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/a/55a64b33750afe51aa0bbd57df78838b96ddd094.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\quad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"2\" />~Minor edit by Quetzalcoatlus\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose the desired angle is some fraction <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> of the total degree measure of the circle. We now compile a list of the shaded and unshaded areas. The inner circle of radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is completely unshaded, so it contributes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to the unshaded area. (Everything will be a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, so we omit it.) The inner annulus has area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/0/560db6d44bdd08f120bf51b515ae2e3aa25b5125.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^2 - 1^2 = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" />, which it contributes to the shaded area. The outer annulus has a total area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/d/41d1a3ea0941d232e795e44e425ead8d13db65e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3^2 - 2^2 = 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" />; the fraction <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is shaded, so the shaded portion of the outer annulus contributes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/e/87e9f9eb8f39820b3b8cd2c4b06e10d90e0d3b44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5x$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> to the shaded area, while the other <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/e/f0e7a14bbc89d0ef16a416c586d2dfd97ffd94e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 - x$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"41\" height=\"12\" /> fraction is unshaded, so the unshaded portion contributes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/4/d44ee5973fe0ce917a38c279e525281b7503f810.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5(1-x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"67\" height=\"20\" /> to the unshaded area. We now equate and solve. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/8/7d8b57c7ba5f4655d27514fd5916e0b2687cce37.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1 + 5(1-x) = 3 + 5x\\]\" width=\"169\" height=\"18\" /> Upon solving, we find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/d/06d116cfbfa69e82f544d1e329b26fc7bf2cffd3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac{3}{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"59\" height=\"38\" />, so the degree measure is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/b/a0beb04d1827d4c63f07610d5a06a3ab4905d024.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$360 \\cdot \\frac{3}{10} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(A)} 108}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"156\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>~ <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:Cxsmi\">cxsmi</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/3/8339ea4baaa51bf6a6876c15321976b035fbbeaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta = \\angle{BOC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"91\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36c7f3f828cf12ab9cafeffad282468a95219600.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> be the area of the inner annulus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/a/e3a8ae66ded7f33f9385a37c9cadfb82720013d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> be the area of the outer annulus sector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/d/76d9b534de592b2a7fc63b97e1f0faab799204e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BOC$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/b/87bc3890c64a3231539079957d49c045fe3f69c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> the total area of the shaded regions. Then,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/c/e4c52aed8457365fbebabac8fe0b1c2e6c24a532.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A_1 + A_2 = A_s\\]\" width=\"107\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/b/87bc3890c64a3231539079957d49c045fe3f69c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> is half the total area so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/1/761ca8f765fb863f9e3f412bf4073a4aff720989.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_s = \\frac{\\pi\\cdot3^2}{2} = \\frac{9\\pi}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"140\" height=\"41\" /> <br />\nThe area of an annulus with outer radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and inner radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is given by:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/7/5b70f33bd927a4868c25dd734f447302e3c4f850.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A = \\pi R^2 - \\pi r^2 = \\pi(R^2-r^2)\\]\" width=\"227\" height=\"20\" />\nSo the area of the inner annulus is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/f/24f8a962434b3401882ee48ea750480f8bcc8e99.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1 = \\pi (2^2 - 1^2) = 3 \\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"172\" height=\"21\" /> <br />\nThe area of the outer annulus sector is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/a/eba7986c48b2e00a6fb0c8c779a2d55519851b8d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A_2 = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ}\\times\\pi(3^2-2^2) = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ}\\times5\\pi\\]\" width=\"291\" height=\"37\" />\nThen we have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/a/1fa4ba43e3e0aa1cfc2af630facd8952f113b367.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3\\pi + \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ}\\times 5 \\pi = \\frac{9\\pi}{2}\\]\" width=\"170\" height=\"37\" />\nSolving for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/1/f51ed5de4af4791d005a87bac8bd340b1d3e3a90.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ}\\times 5 \\pi = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\]\" width=\"126\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/7/d77f696a661775f1b06c5826f167fe2604dd2b05.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\theta = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\times\\frac{360^\\circ}{5\\pi}\\]\" width=\"116\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/3/cd3bfb2b724e84783c80b87b38e9813168f62324.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\theta = \\frac{3\\times360^\\circ}{10}\\]\" width=\"101\" height=\"37\" />\nSo our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/2/6526827c269a68a66dce896cca164517225cb7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta = 3\\times36^\\circ, \\boxed{(A) 108}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"167\" height=\"32\" />\n</p><p>~Seafowl23\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/OwJvuq6F7sQ\">https://youtu.be/OwJvuq6F7sQ</a>\n</p><p>~Thesmartgreekmathdude\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_DDM\">Video Solution by DDM</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by DDM\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/29xcfUcmTgg\">https://youtu.be/29xcfUcmTgg</a>\n</p><p>~piggydude\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_Math-X_.28First_fully_understand_the_problem.21.21.21.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_Math-X_(First_fully_understand_the_problem!!!)\">Video Solution 1 by Math-X (First fully understand the problem!!!)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by Math-X (First fully understand the problem!!!)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/BaE00H2SHQM?si=lg7OGcJ7OwdDFHAn&amp;t=4872\">https://youtu.be/BaE00H2SHQM?si=lg7OGcJ7OwdDFHAn&amp;t=4872</a>\n</p><p>~Math-X\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_2_.28A_Clever_Explanation_You.E2.80.99ll_Get_Instantly.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2_(A_Clever_Explanation_You’ll_Get_Instantly)\">Video Solution 2 (A Clever Explanation You’ll Get Instantly)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2 (A Clever Explanation You’ll Get Instantly)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/5ZIFnqymdDQ?si=A8VVtUidVZlXDyrN&amp;t=2517\">https://youtu.be/5ZIFnqymdDQ?si=A8VVtUidVZlXDyrN&amp;t=2517</a>\n</p><p>~hsnacademy\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_3_.28super_clear.21.29_by_Power_Solve\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_3_(super_clear!)_by_Power_Solve\">Video Solution 3 (super clear!) by Power Solve</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 3 (super clear!) by Power Solve\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/TlTN7EQcFvE\">https://youtu.be/TlTN7EQcFvE</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_4_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution 4 by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 4 by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Svibu3nKB7E\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Svibu3nKB7E</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_5_by_NiuniuMaths_.28Easy_to_understand.21.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_5_by_NiuniuMaths_(Easy_to_understand!)\">Video Solution 5 by NiuniuMaths (Easy to understand!)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 5 by NiuniuMaths (Easy to understand!)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V-xN8Njd_Lc\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V-xN8Njd_Lc</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_Central_Valley_Math_Circle_.28Goes_through_full_thought_process.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Central_Valley_Math_Circle_(Goes_through_full_thought_process)\">Video Solution by Central Valley Math Circle (Goes through full thought process)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=14\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Central Valley Math Circle (Goes through full thought process)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/JZx2-QX--Os\">https://youtu.be/JZx2-QX--Os</a>\n</p><p>~mr_mathman\n</p><p>~NiuniuMaths\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_6_by_OmegaLearn.org_.28Easy_To_Understand.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_6_by_OmegaLearn.org_(Easy_To_Understand)\">Video Solution 6 by OmegaLearn.org (Easy To Understand)</span><span class=\"mw-edi